A binding names a value.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    score = 10
    player_name = "Priya"
]

Here `score` holds an `I32` and `player_name` holds a `String`. When you leave
the type off a numeric literal, Moth can use an exact type from elsewhere in
the same function, for example from a parameter that receives the value.
Without that information, a whole number defaults to `I32`. A number with
a decimal point or an exponent defaults to `F64`. See
@../numbers/#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) for the inference rules.

Write the type when it makes the code clearer or gives the compiler needed
context:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    score I32 = 10
    player_name String = "Priya"
]

Bindings stay immutable unless you mark them as mutable. An immutable binding
can't take a different value later.
